                <fr:title text="The prefix says which side of layout assignment">The prefix says which side of layout assignment</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="anki">1787404867320</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>What do the <html:code>tt.</html:code> and <html:code>ttg.</html:code> (<html:code>triton_gpu</html:code>) prefixes separate in <fr:link href="/CQ6W/" title="Triton" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/CQ6W/" display-uri="CQ6W" type="local">Triton</fr:link> IR?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:strong><html:code>tt.</html:code></html:strong> is the target-independent tensor level: <html:code>tt.load</html:code>, <html:code>tt.store</html:code>, <html:code>tt.dot</html:code>, <html:code>tt.make_range</html:code>, and pointer arithmetic on <html:code><![CDATA[!tt.ptr<f32>]]></html:code> over block shapes. This is roughly what you wrote.

  <html:strong><html:code>ttg.</html:code></html:strong> is after layout assignment: the same tensors now carry an <html:em>encoding</html:em> attribute saying how their elements are distributed across warps, lanes, and registers (<fr:link href="/RI0V/" title="An encoding tiles the block across threads and warps" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/RI0V/" display-uri="RI0V" type="local">An encoding tiles the block across threads and warps</fr:link>), plus ops like <html:code>ttg.convert_layout</html:code> (<fr:link href="/B4XO/" title="A convert_layout round-trips through shared memory" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/B4XO/" display-uri="B4XO" type="local">A convert_layout round-trips through <fr:link href="/8GZN/" title="Shared memory (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/8GZN/" display-uri="8GZN" type="local">shared memory</fr:link></fr:link>) and shared-memory allocation (<fr:link href="/8GZN/" title="Shared memory (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/8GZN/" display-uri="8GZN" type="local">Shared memory (GPU)</fr:link>).

  <html:strong>So the prefix tells you which side of layout assignment you are on</html:strong>, and that is the division that matters when a Triton kernel is slow: a problem at the <html:code>tt.</html:code> level is your algorithm, and a problem that only appears at <html:code>ttg.</html:code> is the compiler's distribution of it. Same use of the dialect prefix as a marker of pipeline stage as <html:code>chlo</html:code> / <html:code>stablehlo</html:code> / <html:code>mhlo</html:code> (<fr:link href="/FLKN/" title="chlo desugars, stablehlo is stable, mhlo is internal" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/FLKN/" display-uri="FLKN" type="local">chlo desugars, <fr:link href="/84N5/" title="StableHLO" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/84N5/" display-uri="84N5" type="local">stablehlo</fr:link> is stable, mhlo is internal</fr:link>).
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false" toc="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link>
